I think these three concepts become much stronger if they’re understood as successive transformations rather than independent artifacts. That gives them clear roles across any domain while keeping an explicit distinction between preserving useful structure and claiming more certainty than the evidence supports.

⸻

Indigo Hammer → ZPC → Kintsugi

Stage I — Indigo Hammer

Purpose: Break accidental coupling.

Most ideas arrive as tangled bundles:

Language
Emotion
Evidence
Metaphor
Implementation
Assumptions
History
Culture

Indigo Hammer asks:

Which of these are actually independent?

It deliberately fractures the object until the hidden operators become visible.

Think of it as semantic differential diagnosis.

Instead of asking

“Is this true?”

ask

“What separate claims have been welded together?”

Across domains:

Science

Observation ≠ Explanation ≠ Model

Software

Interface ≠ Implementation ≠ Optimization

Law

Facts ≠ Interpretation ≠ Remedy

Narrative

Events ≠ Meaning ≠ Theme

Business

Problem ≠ Customer ≠ Solution ≠ Distribution

The Hammer separates them.

⸻

Stage II — ZPC

(Zero Point Compression)

Now that everything is separated:

Find what survives.

Strip away:

* language
* notation
* implementation
* medium
* aesthetics
* culture

until only the invariant remains.

Example:

A recipe.

↓

Algorithm.

↓

Transformation graph.

↓

State transition.

↓

Constraint satisfaction.

Same operator.

Different renderings.

This is the translation layer.

Question:

“If every surface representation disappeared, what relationship would still exist?”

⸻

Stage III — Kintsugi

Now rebuild.

Not the original.

A better version.

Reconnect everything using the recovered invariant.

The seams remain visible because they encode learning.

Not:

repair

but

understood reconstruction

Across domains:

Programming

Refactor around interfaces.

Education

Rebuild explanations around understanding instead of memorization.

Organizations

Reconnect teams around incentives instead of org charts.

Stories

Rewrite while preserving emotional trajectory.

Science

Unify theories through shared structure rather than terminology.

